Q: JS changing a DOM element&#39;s style transition animation doesn&#39;t work A: <p>It's almost not worth writing a response, since the problem is that you omitted a statement in the second function, but since I encountered some “strange” behavior...</p>
<p>I took the liberty of modifying some parameters (<strong>run1</strong> enlarges while <strong>run2</strong> shrinks) to show that both work.
<div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const dom = document.getElementById( "dom" );

const run1 = () =&gt; { // Good
    dom.style.width = "40px";
    dom.style.transition = "width 1300ms ease";
     dom.offsetHeight;
    dom.style.width = "80px";
};

const run2 = () =&gt; { // Good too... :)
    dom.style.width = "80px";
    dom.style.transition = "width 1300ms ease";

      // add lost line
    dom.offsetHeight;
    requestAnimationFrame( () =&gt; {
       dom.style.width = "40px";
    });
};

setTimeout( run1, 3000 );
setTimeout( run2, 6000 );</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code> #dom {
    display: inline-block;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 12px;
    margin: 6px 0;
    background: #e6f0ff;
    border-radius: 6px;
    font-size: 16px;
  }</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!doctype html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;

&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta charset="utf-8" /&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;document&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;

&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;div id="dom"&gt;dom&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;

&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>And here's the “alienating” behavior: if, instead of adding the missing statement before the <strong>requestAnimationFrame()</strong>, I add any statement inside it, it works again, but if I try the same thing in <strong>run1</strong>, it doesn't work. Can anyone explain this?</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const dom = document.getElementById( "dom" );

const run1 = () =&gt; { // Good
    dom.style.width = "40px";
    dom.style.transition = "width 1300ms ease";
     dom.offsetHeight;
    dom.style.width = "80px";
};

const run2 = () =&gt; { // Good too... :)
    dom.style.width = "80px";
    dom.style.transition = "width 1300ms ease";

      // not add lost line
      // dom.offsetHeight;
    requestAnimationFrame( () =&gt; {

         // but add a sentence
       let a = 5;
       dom.style.width = "40px";
    });
};

setTimeout( run1, 3000 );
setTimeout( run2, 6000 );</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code> #dom {
    display: inline-block;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 12px;
    margin: 6px 0;
    background: #e6f0ff;
    border-radius: 6px;
    font-size: 16px;
  }</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!doctype html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;

&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta charset="utf-8" /&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;document&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;

&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;div id="dom"&gt;dom&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;

&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Note: in the CSS, add the line <strong>display: inline-block;</strong> to prevent the element from occupying the entire width of the page.</p>
<p>#Edited with “mea culpa”:</p>
<p>The previous answer was based on “trial and error” rather than internalizing how <strong>requestAnimationFrame</strong> works.</p>
<p>The problem with the code in <strong>run2</strong> is that you are mixing two different and incompatible ideas. The use of <strong>requestAnimationFrame</strong> is precisely to avoid <strong>setTimeout</strong>, and thus achieve smoother animations.<br />
Let's see how you should implement it:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const dom = document.getElementById( "dom" );
let requestID;
let size = 40;

const run2 = () =&gt; { 
    size += 1;
    dom.style.width = size + "px";
    if( size &lt; 80 )
        requestID = requestAnimationFrame( run2 );
    else {
        cancelAnimationFrame( requestID ); // stop animation
    }
};


const run1 = () =&gt; {    
   requestID = requestAnimationFrame( run2 );
}

setTimeout( run1, 3000 );</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code> #dom {
    display: inline-block;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 12px;
    margin: 6px 0;
    background: #e6f0ff;
    border-radius: 6px;
    font-size: 16px;
  }</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!doctype html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;

&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta charset="utf-8" /&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;document&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;

&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;div id="dom"&gt;dom&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;

&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>The syntax is a little “strange.” <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/requestAnimationFrame" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Here</a> is the source so you can check it out.</p>

Q: How can I generate a pulse of width less than one microseconds in arduino? A: <p>I think the best way to do this is using an RC (resistor capacitor) circuit to flash the eeprom. If you have an arduino, then you should have the components to make an RC circuits since they are so easy and cost effective. If not, you could try port manipulation like below:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#define WE_BIT 2 // PB2 = D10

void setup() {
  DDRB |= (1 &lt;&lt; WE_BIT); // Set D10 as output
  PORTB |= (1 &lt;&lt; WE_BIT); // WE HIGH
}

inline void pulseWE() {

  PORTB &amp;= ~(1 &lt;&lt; WE_BIT); // WE LOW

  __asm__ __volatile__(
    &quot;nop\n\t&quot;
    &quot;nop\n\t&quot;
    &quot;nop\n\t&quot;
    &quot;nop\n\t&quot;
    &quot;nop\n\t&quot;
  );

  PORTB |= (1 &lt;&lt; WE_BIT); // WE HIGH
}

void loop() {
}
</code></pre>

Q: swipe action on desktop via mouse slide action A: <p>By &quot;mouse <em>slide</em>&quot; do you mean drag&amp;drop? I'm not aware of browsers scrolling upon mouse drag&amp;drop, so here's an approach with JS.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>let swiper = document.getElementsByClassName('swiper')[0];
swiper.onmousedown = e =&gt; {
  swipeStartX = e.clientX + swiper.scrollLeft;
  document.addEventListener('mousemove', swipe);
  e.preventDefault();
  swiper.style.scrollSnapType = 'none';
  swiper.style.scrollBehavior = 'auto';
};
document.addEventListener('mouseup', () =&gt; stopSwipe());
window.addEventListener('blur', () =&gt; stopSwipe());
function swipe(e) {
  swiper.scrollLeft = swipeStartX - e.clientX;
}
function stopSwipe() {
  // most mouse moves don't drag, so we avoid having listeners all the time
  document.removeEventListener('mousemove', swipe);
  swiper.style.scrollBehavior = 'smooth';
  requestAnimationFrame(() =&gt; requestAnimationFrame(() =&gt; swiper.style.scrollSnapType = 'x mandatory'));
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.swiper
{
    display: flex;
    overflow-x: scroll;
    scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
    scroll-behavior: smooth;
    -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;

    gap: 0;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0 auto;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 600px;
    overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
}

.swiper::-webkit-scrollbar
{
    display: none;
}

.slide {
    flex: 0 0 100%;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
    scroll-snap-stop: always;

    height: 384px;
    background: #fff;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    font-size: 3rem;
    border-radius: 12px;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://unpkg.com/alpinejs@3.15.2/dist/cdn.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;div class="swiper" x-data=""&gt;
    &lt;template x-for="(item, index) in [...Array(84).keys()].map(i =&gt; i + 1)"&gt;        
        &lt;div class="slide"&gt;
            &lt;img :src=`https://raw.githubusercontent.com/anjanesh/Slide/refs/heads/master/moorthy/img${index}.png` alt="fig" width="512" height="384" /&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/template&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>I override <code>scroll-snap-type</code> and <code>scroll-behavior</code> during dragging so they don't interfere, but restoring <code>scroll-behavior: smooth</code> after dragging doesn't affect the first snap after dragging (even if I postpone the snap by a frame so that <code>scroll-behavior: smooth</code> definitely is in effect before the snap). Maybe that can be a separate question. If that's impossible, JS can animate a smooth snap before <code>scroll-snap-type: x mandatory</code> gets restored for trackpad events.</p>
<p><code>mousemove</code> and <code>mouseup</code> events outside the Stack Snippets <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> get ignored, but they work outside the viewport and browser window if it's not in an <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code>.</p>

Q: Can I use unique_ptr with inherited classes? A: <blockquote>
<p>Can I use unique_ptr with inherited classes</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes.<br />
<code>std::unique_ptr</code> supports using the pointer polymorphically, similarly to a raw pointer (and this is relevant for class members as well).<br />
For C++11, you can initialize/assign the smart pointer from a <code>new B</code> or <code>new C</code>.</p>
<p>This is demonstrated below:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;memory&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

struct A { 
    virtual ~A(){}   // virtual destructor is required !!!
    virtual void do_something() {} 
};

struct B : public A { 
    void do_something() override { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B::do_something()\n&quot;; }
};

struct C : public A {
    void do_something() override { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;C::do_something()\n&quot;; }
};

class Foo
{
public:
    void someFunction(bool cond)
    {
        if (cond) {
            // Create a B:
            a = std::unique_ptr&lt;A&gt;(new B);
        }
        else {
            // Create a C:
            a = std::unique_ptr&lt;A&gt;(new C);
        }
        // Invoke a virtual method:
        a-&gt;do_something();
    }
private:
    std::unique_ptr&lt;A&gt; a;
};

int main() {
    Foo foo;
    foo.someFunction(true);
    foo.someFunction(false);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>B::do_something()
C::do_something()
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/aGaeYaf4T" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live demo 1</a>.</p>
<p>If you can use C++14 or higher, there's a better option to initialize the smart pointer with <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/memory/unique_ptr/make_unique" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::make_unique</code></a>:</p>
<pre><code>a = std::make_unique&lt;B&gt;();
// or:
a = std::make_unique&lt;C&gt;();
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/8cG3jW363" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live demo 2</a>.</p>
<p>Finally, as @j6t commented below, you can use <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/memory/unique_ptr/reset.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::unique_ptr::reset()</code></a> to assign the smart pointer to manage a new object of class <code>A</code> or any derived, e.g.:</p>
<pre><code>a.reset(new B);
// or:
a.reset(new C);
</code></pre>

Q: locator.filter works with hardcoded value but not variable A: <p>Unfortunately, there's no apparent way to pass data into <code>.filter()</code> as there is with <code>page.evaluate()</code>. See <a href="https://github.com/puppeteer/puppeteer/issues/11893" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this issue</a>. But as <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79532494/puppeteer-case-insensitive-text-locator-with-variable-from-node-scope/79532655#79532655">Puppeteer case insensitive text locator with variable from Node scope</a> shows,
you can use string concatenation:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const l = 'LOGIN';
await page.locator('a, button').filter(`e =&gt; e.innerText === '${l}'`).click();
</code></pre>
<p>If a substring is permissible, you might consider</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// hardcoded &quot;LOGIN&quot; text
await page.locator('a ::-p-text(LOGIN), button ::-p-text(LOGIN)').click();
</code></pre>
<p>or</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const l = 'LOGIN';
await page.locator(`a ::-p-text(${l}), button ::-p-text(${l})`).click();
</code></pre>
<p>The fairly uncommon <code>,</code> CSS operator results in some unfortunate duplication here. See <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/64626061/6243352">this post</a> for more &quot;find by text&quot; options in Puppeteer.</p>
<p>Here's a complete, runnable example:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const puppeteer = require(&quot;puppeteer&quot;); // ^24.36.1

const html = `&lt;button onclick=&quot;f()&quot;&gt;LOGIN&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;script&gt;function f() {
  const b = document.querySelector(&quot;button&quot;);
  b.textContent = &quot;clicked!&quot;;
}&lt;/script&gt;`;

let browser;
(async () =&gt; {
  browser = await puppeteer.launch();
  const [page] = await browser.pages();
  await page.setContent(html);
  const text = 'LOGIN';
  await page.locator(`a ::-p-text(${text}), button ::-p-text(${text})`).click();
  console.log(await page.$eval(&quot;button&quot;, el =&gt; el.textContent)); // =&gt; clicked!
})()
  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(err))
  .finally(() =&gt; browser?.close());
</code></pre>
<p>Nits: <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/35213147/difference-between-textcontent-vs-innertext">prefer <code>.textContent</code> to <code>.innerText</code></a>, trim the HTML when using <code>===</code>, and avoid <code>l</code> as a variable name. It's too easy to confuse with <code>1</code> or <code>I</code>.</p>
<p>Another approach for completeness is using</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const text = 'LOGIN';
await page.evaluate(text =&gt; window.__text = text, text);
await page
  .locator('a, button')
  .filter(e =&gt; e.innerText === window.__text)
  .click();
</code></pre>
<p>to attach the dynamic value to the window, accessible in future operations. This is a bit brittle though, only mentioned for completeness if string concatenation won't work.</p>
<p>You can follow <a href="https://github.com/puppeteer/puppeteer/issues/11893" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this issue</a> to see if Puppeteer winds up improving this in the future.</p>
